<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>8.5.1: Reviewing Zero Product Property</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02159</md:content-id>
    <md:title>8.5.1: Reviewing Zero Product Property</md:title>
    <md:uuid>49d59bc1-4d14-485e-ae56-d9c3adf5b551</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>Decide whether each statement is true or false.</para>
<section class="check-understanding"><title>Warm Up Activity</title><exercise data-content-id="6992756e-5681-457f-b6a7-c2e9550f74e2" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>3 is the only solution to \(x^2−9=0\).</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! False. There are two solutions because there are two numbers that can be squared to get 9. Both 3 and –3 are possible solutions.</para><para>False. There are two solutions because there are two numbers that can be squared to get 9. Both 3 and –3 are possible solutions.</para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="4f56e224-75d6-447b-8675-412ccf6c4c52" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>A solution to \(x^2+25=0\) is –5. </para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! False. There is no solution to \(x^2+25=0\) because no real number can be squared to get –25.</para><para>False. There is no solution to \(x^2+25=0\) because no real number can be squared to get –25.</para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="a84ff741-2f65-4dcd-a5eb-bc0584d6f53a" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>\(x(x−7)=0\) has two solutions.</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! True. There are two factors, 0 and 7, that could make the product of the expression 0.</para><para>True. There are two factors, 0 and 7, that could make the product of the expression 0.</para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="e4012223-6761-4182-9aa8-3f81e734d6b5" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>5 and –7 are the solutions to \((x−5)(x+7)=12\).</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! False. The product of the two factors is not 0, so we cannot solve it using the zero product property (which gives 5 and –7 as solutions). If we evaluate the expression \((x−5)(x+7)\) at those two values, the result will be 0, not 12.</para><para>False. The product of the two factors is not 0, so we cannot solve it using the zero product property (which gives 5 and –7 as solutions). If we evaluate the expression \((x−5)(x+7)\) at those two values, the result will be 0, not 12.</para></solution></exercise></section></section>
</content>
